=begin pod :pubdate('2026-09-23 05:00:00') :puburl</blog/2026-09-23-mcp-server-raku-answer>
=TITLE Connect your AI agent to the Raku knowledge base over MCP
=begin DESCRIPTION
Following my own install page, and the one correction it produced
=end DESCRIPTION

=head1 From a file to a source

An AI agent will answer a Raku question with the same confidence whether it read
the docs or remembered them wrong. Naming a source is not enough either — a real
page can be cited for something it does not say. So I asked one for the page,
the section, and the passage it was leaning on: how does an action class share
state between grammar rules?

I searched the collection and opened the L<grammars page|https://raku-knowledge-base.podlite.org/doc/language/grammars>,
then the section I expected. Wrong one, and I knew it within seconds. The answer was under B<"Always succeed" assertion>N<That section sits under
B<Special tokens> on the same page, between B<sym> and B<Methods in grammars>.>, in the C<Digifier> and
C<Devanagari> example: the action class declares C<has @!numbers>. C<succ> pushes a new
empty element and C<digit> appends to the last one, so both methods write to the
same array. That is how the state gets from one rule to the next.

Next to it, B<Attributes in grammars> adds a limit worth knowing. Grammar
attributes can only be reached from methods, and changing one inside a method
called from a token changes it for that token's own match object alone.

The answer is small. I can still trace every step that produced it: question,
page, section, the example itself. I guessed the wrong section first, and the
collection made that easy to see.

=head1 Where the collection ends

What it reads is the document collection made available through
L<the knowledge base|https://raku-knowledge-base.podlite.org/>: the Raku
documentation, module documentation from both ecosystems — C<p6c> and C<zef> —
and the examples repository. It does not necessarily include every page on the
site.

Ask it about Raku and it answers. Ask it about the tooling around Raku and you
find the edge quickly. I searched it for the offline bundle that ships with this
very server. Back came the Raku MCP modules — L<MCP|https://raku-knowledge-base.podlite.org/mods/zef/MCP>,
L<MCP::Server|https://raku-knowledge-base.podlite.org/mods/zef/MCP::Server>, L<MCP::Client|https://raku-knowledge-base.podlite.org/mods/zef/MCP::Client> — none of
which package anything. After them came modules that had simply matched my
words: L<Sparrowdo::Ruby::Bundler|https://raku-knowledge-base.podlite.org/mods/all/Sparrowdo::Ruby::Bundler> drives
Ruby's Bundler from Raku, and L<App::InstallerMaker::WiX|https://raku-knowledge-base.podlite.org/mods/zef/App::InstallerMaker::WiX>
builds MSI installers for Raku applications.

Every hit was Raku. None of them was about packaging this server, because the
collection does not hold that.

=begin nested :notify<caution> :caption('A miss is not an absence')
When this service finds nothing, that is a fact about the collection, not about
Raku. Check the site before you conclude the documentation does not cover it.
=end nested

=head1 Installing it

Before writing this I ran the install myself, and not on the setup I had been
using all along. Fresh bundle from
L<release data-2026-09-11|https://github.com/zag/raku-knowledge-base/releases/tag/data-2026-09-11>, wired into Codex
— not Claude Code, which the page was written for. It found the grammars page. It
named B<"Always succeed" assertion> and quoted the passage back.

It travels as C<raku-kb-mcp-offline.tar.gz>, attached to every data
release, with the search index already built. These three lines are the ones I
ran for that release; L<the MCP page|https://raku-knowledge-base.podlite.org/mcp>
keeps the current ones:

=begin code :lang<shell>
tar xzf raku-kb-mcp-offline.tar.gz
cd raku-kb-mcp && npm install
claude mcp add raku-kb -- node "$PWD/bin/raku-kb-mcp.mjs"
=end code

The third line is for Claude Code; other clients point at the same file with
their own command or config. The page has both, and a prompt you can paste to
check the connection.

=begin nested :notify<tip> :caption('If you script the check')
A client running with no one to approve tool calls may never reach the server
and will report that it cannot answer. That is the approval policy, not the
collection. In Codex, C<--approve-for-me> let the call through.
=end nested

That run exposed one correction. The page used to ask the agent for the
lines it relied on, and the server returns page text without line numbers. It
asks for a quote now.

=head1 What actually changes

The amount of text the agent can see stays the same. In the grammar run it went from
question to page to section to the lines of an example, and every step was
visible — including the one where I had guessed wrong. When the source is there,
I can check it. When it isn't, the agent has a plain reason to say it
doesn't know.
=end pod
